  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 230 PM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nSynoptically, not much has changed; the ridge that has been in place \nover the Four Corners region is still likely to stay nearly \nstationary over the next week. By Saturday, guidance is starting to \nsignal that the ridge will begin to de-amplify as a trough moves \nalong the US/Canada border. Throughout the week, there should be a \nfew shortwaves moving through the area. \n\nHigh temperatures for the week look to \"cool\" down to the mid to \nhigh 90s. Winds look to remain calm to breezy, with minimum relative \nhumidity values staying above 25%. \n\nMoving to precipitation chances for the period: \n\nTuesday's PoP's are currently forecast to be in the 30-60% range, \nwith the highest percentages in eastern Colorado. Guidance indicates \nthat the surface low just to the south of the CWA will likely remain \nthere throughout the day. With the low stationary, winds should \nmainly be from the east-northeast, promoting upslope flow for \nshowers/storms. After looking at instability and shear for \nsevere storm potential, there are signs that if storms were \ndevelop, they would mainly stay on the marginal side. For the \nnorthwestern portion of the CWA, there is a 25-40% chance of \nexceeding 0.10\" of precipitation from the afternoon into early \nWednesday morning. \n\nWednesday still looks to have higher precipitation chances of the \nlong-term period as a shortwave propagates through during the \nafternoon and evening. Guidance indicates a boundary moving in from \nthe northwest toward the southeast, though models disagree on its \nexact location and timing. Currently, both the GFS and Canadian \nmodels show the boundary; however, the GFS brings it in late \nafternoon, whereas the Canadian delays it until early Thursday \nmorning. Other guidance doesn't have the boundary. If it does \nmaterialize, severe storm potential would be marginal. PoPs are \ncurrently forecast to be 40-65% where once again eastern Colorado \nhaving the highest percentages. Eastern Colorado currently has a \n25\u201345% chance of exceeding 0.10\" of precipitation. Given this is \nroughly four days out, it will be interesting to see how guidance \ntrends for the location and speed of the boundary. \n\nFor the rest of the week and weekend, PoPs steadily decrease. \nThursday\u2019s chances are around 20\u201330%, and Friday through Sunday \nstays around 10-20% each day. However, with shortwaves passing \nthrough, any favorable lifting mechanism, like a boundary or front, \ncould nudge these precipitation chances up or down as we get\ncloser.",
